  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "The first term of a geometric series is $10$, the last term is $\\frac{10}{81}$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the sum of this series?\n\nExpress your answer as a fraction (do not round your answer).\n\nThe sum of this series is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio. In our case, we have ${a}_{1}=10$ and $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nWe must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{81} & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{81} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{N-1} \\\\ 4 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=10,$ $r=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =10\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{2}{3})}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{5}-1}{{3}^{5}} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{242}{243} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,210}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
